WEBVTT

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:03.310
What do you do with a stack of interview
transcripts?

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.950
Think of a codebook as a map legend.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.450
It helps you mark what you see the same
way as you move through the material.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:14.085
Imagine a rider who says a transit alert
arrived on time, but they couldn’t tell

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:14.930
where to transfer.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:20.385
Mark that passage “next step is unclear.”
The codebook should say when to use

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.090
that label and when to leave it out.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.950
An alert that came too late needs its own
label.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:26.970
Then compare interviews.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:31.585
If several riders had time to act but
didn’t know where to go, the theme might

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.930
be that the notice gives them time
without giving them enough direction.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:36.430
Check the exceptions.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.090
One rider might have found a posted map.

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:43.865
That gives the team a concrete next step,
like testing a notice that names the stop

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:44.970
and includes a map.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.930
Need to turn interview evidence into a
decision?

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.665
Visit prcimpact.com or call

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:54.470
(334) 429-6994.
